{:edb/db-before-t 2103 :edb/db-after-t 2104 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535416 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:41:30.104Z" 13194139535416 true] [17592186087525 1100 "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 10, 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 10,20\\rangle$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186087525 1100 "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 10, 0\\rangle$.\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 10,20\\rangle$ when $t=1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t$." 13194139535416 false] [17592186087525 1145 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨10, 0⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫10dt, ∫0dt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨10t + C_{1}, C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} (1)= \\langle 10,20\\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(1) &= ⟨10(1) + C_{1}, C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨10, 20⟩ &= ⟨10 + C_{1}, C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 &= 10 + C_{1} \\\\\nC_{1} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n$C_2 = 20$.\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ⟨10t, 20⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535416 false] [17592186087525 1145 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨10,0⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 10\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 0\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10t+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v} (1)= \\langle 10,20\\rangle.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨10(1)+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨10,20⟩ & =⟨10+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =10+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nC_2 = 20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨10t,\\,20⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186087527 1100 "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 2e^{2t}, 3e^{3t}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle e^2, 2e^3 \\rangle$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=2.$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186087527 1100 "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 2e^{2t}, 3e^{3t}\\rangle$.\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle e^2, 2e^3 \\rangle$ when $t=1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=2$." 13194139535416 false] [17592186087527 1145 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨2e^{2t}, 3e^{3t}⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫2e^{2t}dt, ∫3e^{3t}dt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{2t} + C_{1}, e^{3t} + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(1) = \\langle e^2, 2e^3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(1) &= ⟨e^{2(1)} + C_{1}, e^{3(1)} + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨e^{2}, 2e^{3}⟩ &= ⟨e^{2} + C_{1}, e^{3} + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=0$ and $C_2 = e^3$. Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ⟨e^{2t}, e^{3t} + e^{3}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(2) &= ⟨e^{2(2)}, e^{3(2)} + e^{3}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{4}, e^{6} + e^{3}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535416 false] [17592186087527 1145 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2{e}^{2t},3{e}^{3t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 3{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v}(1) = \\langle e^2, 2e^3 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨{e}^{2(1)}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{3(1)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{2},2{e}^{3}⟩ & =⟨{e}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=0$ and $C_2 = e^3.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{e}^{2t},\\,{e}^{3t}+{e}^{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨{e}^{2(2)},\\,{e}^{3(2)}+{e}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{4},\\,{e}^{6}+{e}^{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186087528 1100 "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 1, 2t \\rangle, \\quad -\\infty <        t <        \\infty.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 2,0 \\rangle$ when $t=1,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139535416 true] [17592186087528 1100 "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$\\mathbf{a}(t) = \\langle 1, 2t \\rangle, \\quad -\\infty < t < \\infty$.\nGiven that the particle has velocity $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 2,0 \\rangle$ when $t=1$, calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139535416 false] [17592186087528 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫⟨1, 2t⟩dt \\\\\n&= ⟨∫1dt, ∫2tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t + C_{1}, t^{2} + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} (1)= \\langle 2,0\\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(1) &= ⟨1 + C_{1}, 1^{2} + C_{2}⟩ \\\\\n⟨2, 0⟩ &= ⟨1 + C_{1}, 1 + C_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=1$ and $C_2=-1$. Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ⟨t + 1, t^{2} - 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= 0 \\\\\n⟨t + 1, t^{2} - 1⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt + 1 &= 0⇒t =-1, \\\\\nt^{2} - 1 = (t + 1)(t - 1) &= 0⇒t = ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t= -1$, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139535416 false] [17592186087528 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1,2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v} (1)= \\langle 2,0\\rangle.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,{1}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,0⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=1$ and $C_2=-1.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨t+1,\\,{t}^{2}-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨t+1,\\,{t}^{2}-1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t+1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1, \\\\ {t}^{2}-1=(t+1)(t-1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t= -1,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139535416 true] [17592186087704 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348092 13194139535416 true] [17592186087704 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348141 13194139535416 true] [17592186087704 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348146 13194139535416 true] [17592186087704 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348154 13194139535416 true] [17592186087705 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348099 13194139535416 true] [17592186087705 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348106 13194139535416 true] [17592186087705 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348113 13194139535416 true] [17592186087705 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348161 13194139535416 true] [17592186087706 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348120 13194139535416 true] [17592186087706 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348127 13194139535416 true] [17592186087706 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348134 13194139535416 true] [17592186087706 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348151 13194139535416 true] [17592186087706 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348168 13194139535416 true] [17592186088072 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535416 true] [17592186088072 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨8{t}^{3},-1⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,\\,3⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=3.$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186088072 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$a(t) = ⟨8t^{3},-1⟩$.\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v = ⟨ - 1, 3⟩$ when $t = 1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t = 3$." 13194139535416 false] [17592186088072 1145 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8{t}^{3},-1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int -1\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨-1,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨2(1)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,-(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,3⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},\\,-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-3$ and ${C}_{2}=4.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨2{t}^{4}-3,\\,-t+4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨2(3)^{4}-3,\\,-(3)+4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨159,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186088146 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535416 true] [17592186088146 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},{e}^{3t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨{e}^{2},{e}^{3}⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186088146 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$a(t) = ⟨e^{2t}, e^{3t}⟩$.\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v = ⟨e^{2}, e^{3}⟩$ when $t = 1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t$." 13194139535416 false] [17592186088146 1145 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨{e}^{2t},{e}^{3t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨{e}^{2},{e}^{3}⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(1)}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3(1)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{2},{e}^{3}⟩ & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2},\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{3}⟩\\,.\n$$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348092 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348093 13194139535416 true] [17592186348092 1089 "q-122056" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348092 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535416 true] [17592186348092 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},4⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡10,3⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348092 1113 #uuid "8f2a7046-8943-5dcd-b8be-3c711d226765" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348092 1145 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},4⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+{C}_{1},4t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡10,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1)+{C}_{1},4(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡10,3⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{C}_{1},4+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡10 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{2}) \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =4+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡5,4t-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡[5(t+1)],4t-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),4t-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348094 13194139535416 true] [17592186348093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348095 13194139535416 true] [17592186348093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348096 13194139535416 true] [17592186348093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348097 13194139535416 true] [17592186348093 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348098 13194139535416 true] [17592186348093 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535416 true] [17592186348093 1041 "selection" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348093 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348094 13194139535416 true] [17592186348093 1110 #uuid "d92a3946-a88f-5d59-8619-57fb5e4b430d" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348094 1034 "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),4t-1⟩" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348094 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535416 true] [17592186348094 1111 #uuid "34cab502-18f2-5c28-bb54-02e45f00569c" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348095 1034 "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+8),t+2⟩" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348095 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535416 true] [17592186348095 1111 #uuid "2f621e86-d8ef-5fe3-8eca-6cebec5e6248" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348096 1034 "v=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2},4t-1⟩" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348096 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535416 true] [17592186348096 1111 #uuid "1f1857a7-a665-59b6-95f4-ac0f6b6d59d3" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348097 1034 "v=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2},2t+1⟩" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535416 true] [17592186348097 1111 #uuid "f33a0027-478b-576a-9923-5b2f75119578" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348098 1034 "v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5t+5),2t+1⟩" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348098 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535416 true] [17592186348098 1111 #uuid "fd19f564-179f-5de3-8c60-9e3504a5a9bc" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348099 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348100 13194139535416 true] [17592186348099 1089 "q-122093" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348099 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535416 true] [17592186348099 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1,1-t⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨0,1⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=0.$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348099 1113 #uuid "c014a6f8-66f2-56f8-b047-75d428488686" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348099 1145 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨{t}^{2}-1,1-t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int ({t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (1-t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t+{C}_{1},t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(3)=⟨0,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}-3+{C}_{1},3-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & =⟨6+{C}_{1},-\\frac{3}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-6$ and ${C}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t-6,t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}-0-6,0-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,\\frac{5}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348101 13194139535416 true] [17592186348100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348102 13194139535416 true] [17592186348100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348103 13194139535416 true] [17592186348100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348104 13194139535416 true] [17592186348100 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348105 13194139535416 true] [17592186348100 1040 #edb/ref 1759