 time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨-1,2⟩,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,2⟩$ when $t=2,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139535416 true] [17592186348120 1113 #uuid "6f72a972-4df0-59a3-be02-4332225a0edb" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348120 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨-1,2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-t+{C}_{1},2t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨-1,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨-2+{C}_{1},2(2)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨-2+{C}_{1},4+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨1-t,2t-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1-t,2t-2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-t & =0\\,⇒\\,t=1, \\\\ 2t-2=2(t-1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=1,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139535416 true] [17592186348121 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348122 13194139535416 true] [17592186348121 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348123 13194139535416 true] [17592186348121 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348124 13194139535416 true] [17592186348121 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348125 13194139535416 true] [17592186348121 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348126 13194139535416 true] [17592186348121 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535416 true] [17592186348121 1041 "select