ituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨(3)^{3}-5(3)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,(3)^{2}-4(3)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-18,-8⟩ & =⟨-18+{C}_{1},\\,-3+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}-4t-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}-4t-5⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}={t}^{2}(t-5) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0,5, \\\\ {t}^{2}-4t-5=(t+1)(t-5) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=5,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139535416 true] [17592186348135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348136 13194139535416 true] [17592186348135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348137 13194139535416 true] [17592186348135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348138 13194139535416 true] [17592186348135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348139 13194139535416 true] [17592186348135 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348140 13194139535416 true] [17592186348135 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535416 true] [17592186348135 1041 "selection" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348135 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348138 13194139535416 true] [17592186348135 1110 #uuid "08e19ed7-69f5-5c50-a7e4-105d584ce353" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348136 1034 "t=1" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535416 true] [17592186348136 1111 #uuid "f7a77d68-e020-5747-90cb-42e5f1065429" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348137 1034 "t=0" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535416 true] [17592186348137 1111 #uuid "6dbd75f2-f912-5939-b3d6-dde6e3887872" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348138 1034 "t=5" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348138 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535416 true] [17592186348138 1111 #uuid "82