t=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=4.$ So, this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139535416 true] [17592186348152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348153 13194139535416 true] [17592186348152 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535416 true] [17592186348152 1041 "field-1" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348152 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348153 13194139535416 true] [17592186348152 1110 #uuid "de03e589-e6f7-5b8a-935e-30a205008e5e" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348153 1034 "4" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535416 true] [17592186348153 1111 #uuid "3d8584f3-cfeb-5f5e-bff4-1965b9f69633" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348154 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348155 13194139535416 true] [17592186348154 1089 "q-7196" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348154 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535416 true] [17592186348154 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na=⟨1,2⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨4,3⟩$ when $t=1$, calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348154 1113 #uuid "c3c2a344-d3bb-5ceb-a76b-4fdb148ea6a1" 13194139535416 true] [17592186348154 1145 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1,2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+{C}_{1},2t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨4,3⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨1+{C}_{1},2(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,3⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},2+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =1+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =2+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv=⟨t+3,2t+1⟩.\n$$"